course: everyday
lesson: 32
title: Electricity at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Electricity is a flow of tiny charged particles through a path called a circuit. Metal wires carry it easily; plastic and rubber covers stop it from escaping.
explain: Your body can carry electricity too. If it finds a path through you to the ground, it can give a shock that burns, stops the heart or throws you off a ladder.
explain: Water from a tap or a bath is not pure: it has dissolved salts in it, and these let electricity flow through it. Wet skin also lets far more current into the body than dry skin.
explain: For example, a hair dryer that falls into a bath full of water can send current through the water and through the person in it. That is why electrical things are kept away from baths and sinks.
explain: The plastic cover on a cord is its safety coat. When it is frayed, cracked or chewed, the bare wire can touch you, so the cord must not be used until an adult replaces it.
explain: A boy in England flew his kite near overhead power lines; the kite string touched the wires and the electricity went down the string, and he was badly burnt.
explain: A girl in Florida used a hair dryer next to a full bath; it fell in, and only the safety switch in the house tripping in time stopped her getting a deadly shock.
fact: Electricity flows easily through metal, water with salts in it, and the human body.
fact: Plastic and rubber do not let electricity flow easily, so they are used to cover wires.
fact: Wet skin lets much more electricity into the body than dry skin.
fact: You should always dry your hands before touching plugs, switches or electrical appliances.
fact: You must never push anything into a wall socket, especially metal objects like keys or forks.
fact: A frayed or damaged cord should be unplugged by an adult and not used again until it is fixed.
fact: You should pull a plug out by holding the plug itself, not by yanking the cord, because yanking can damage the wires and break their plastic cover.
fact: Plugging too many things into one socket can overheat the wires and start a fire.
fact: If someone is getting an electric shock, you must not touch them; an adult should switch off the power and call for help.
fact: Fallen power lines can still be live, so you must stay far away and tell an adult.
fact: Kites and drones should be flown far from overhead power lines.
fact: Electricity can jump from power lines to a kite, a ladder or a fishing rod and kill the person holding it.
fact: Water and electricity together can give a deadly shock.
exercise: Why should you dry your hands before you switch on a lamp? | Wet skin lets electricity into the body much more easily | Water makes the switch stick so that it will not turn on at all | Wet hands make the bulb burn out faster | Dry hands make the lamp shine more brightly
exercise: Why are the wires inside a cord covered in plastic? | Plastic does not let electricity flow through it easily | Plastic makes the electricity flow faster along the wire to the lamp | Plastic stops the wire from getting cold | Plastic makes the cord bend more easily
exercise: How can tap water carry electricity? | It has dissolved salts that let the current flow | It is made of tiny pieces of metal | It is always charged up by the metal water pipes it runs through | It only carries electricity when it is hot
exercise: Why must you never push a fork into a wall socket? | The metal fork would carry electricity into your body | The fork would crack the socket's plastic cover and break it for good | The socket would melt the fork into liquid | The electricity would only damage the fork
exercise: You notice the cord of a toaster is cracked and you can see wire. What should you do? | Leave it unplugged and tell an adult | Wrap it in sticky tape and keep using it | Use it only for a few more days | Bend the cord so the wire is hidden
exercise: How should you unplug a lamp? | Hold the plug itself and pull it out | Pull hard on the cord from across the room | Pull on the lamp until the plug comes out | Wiggle the cord until the plug drops
exercise: Why can plugging many things into one socket be dangerous? | The wires can overheat and start a fire | The electricity runs out for the whole house | The appliances all start to work more slowly | The socket can no longer turn off at night
exercise: Your friend is stuck to a machine and getting a shock. Why must you not grab them? | The current could pass into your body too | You might pull them in the wrong direction | They will let go by themselves in a moment | Touching them makes the shock stronger for them
exercise: Why should a radio never be placed on the edge of a bath? | If it falls in, the water can carry current through the person | The steam from the bath makes the music too quiet to hear properly | The bath water would stop the radio from working | The radio would make the bath water cold
exercise: A storm brings a power line down in your street. What should you do? | Stay far away and tell an adult at once | Go closer to see if it is still working | Move it off the road with a dry stick | Step over it quickly if you are wearing shoes
exercise: Why should kites be flown far away from power lines? | A kite touching a line could carry electricity down to you | Power lines make the wind too weak for kites | Kites can switch the power off in the street | Power lines pull kites toward them like magnets
exercise: How can a chewed cord be dangerous? | Bare wire can touch your skin and shock you | It makes the appliance use less electricity | It slows the electricity so nothing works | It is only dangerous if the cord is very long
exercise: Which of these lets electricity flow most easily? | A metal spoon | A rubber glove | A plastic ruler | A dry wooden block
test: A boy in Florida has just come out of the pool and wants to plug in the speaker. What should he do first? | Dry his hands and stand away from the wet floor | Hold the plug with wet fingers but do it very quickly | Use his elbow so his hands stay wet | Plug it in while still standing in the pool
test: Which statement is true? | Water from a tap can carry electricity | Only metal can carry electricity | Rubber carries electricity better than copper | The human body never carries electricity
test: A little sister is trying to push a hair clip into a socket. What is the best thing to do? | Stop her gently and tell an adult at once | Let her try because sockets are always off | Help her push it in more slowly | Give her a fork to use instead
test: What would most likely happen if someone kept using a lamp with a badly frayed cord? | They could get a shock or start a fire | The lamp would simply give brighter light | The cord would slowly heal by itself | Nothing, because lamps use little electricity
test: Grandad sees someone being shocked by a broken heater. What is the safe order of actions? | Switch off the power, then help and call for help | Pull the person away with bare hands, then call | Pour water on them, then switch off the power | Wait for them to let go, then switch off
test: Why is it a bad idea to yank a phone charger out by its cord? | It can damage the wires and break the cover | It makes the phone charge more slowly next time | It turns the plug into a magnet | It wastes electricity from the socket
test: A girl in Calgary sees a kite stuck in a power line. What should she do? | Leave it and tell an adult or the power company | Climb the pole to free the kite | Pull the kite string hard to get it down quickly | Throw a stick at the kite to knock it off the wire
test: Why do electricians often wear rubber gloves? | Rubber does not let electricity pass easily | Rubber makes their hands warmer in winter | Rubber pulls electricity out of the wires | Rubber helps them hold the wires tighter
test: Five heaters and a TV are plugged into one strip, and the plug feels hot. What does that warning sign mean? | The wires are overheating and could start a fire | The electricity is working especially well today | The heaters are about to switch off for good | The strip is charging up to store power for later
test: hands wet, gonna plug in the hairdryer | Dry your hands first | Fine, if you're quick | Fine, water stops shocks | Only if it's a small plug
test: toast stuck, can i poke the socket with a fork | Never put anything into a socket | Yes, if it's switched off at the wall | Yes, with a metal fork | Yes, if you're quick
test: power line fell in the street after the storm | Stay far away and tell an adult | Poke it with a stick | It's dead now after the storm, it's safe | Step over it carefully
test: just yank the plug out by the cable | No, pull by the plug itself | Yes, the cable is stronger than the plug | Yes, that's what it's for | Yes, if you pull hard
test: kite's stuck in the power lines, i'll climb up and get it | Never, leave it and tell an adult to call the power company | Yes, if you wear rubber shoes, they stop the electricity | Yes, pull it down with a stick | Yes, if the wires look old
test: there's a power line lying on the road after the storm | Stay far away and tell an adult to call for help | Step over it carefully | Poke it to see if it is live | Move it out of the way with a dry branch from a tree
test: phone charger cable's frayed and sparking a bit | Unplug it at the wall and tell an adult | Tape it up and just keep using it, it's fine | Keep charging, sparks are normal | Wrap it round your hand to hold it
test: my cousin's friend stuck a fork in the plug socket and got thrown across the room, his hair went all spiky | Real lesson: never push anything into a socket; the spiky hair is probably made up | Real lesson: forks are safe if they are plastic-handled | It proves sockets only shock boys | It is gossip, so sockets are harmless
test: they reckon if someone's getting a shock u should pull them off quick, that's what heroes do | Wrong; never touch them, an adult should switch off the power and call for help | They are right, pull them off at once, every second counts | They are right, if you use wet hands | They are right, as long as you are quick
test: the Wilsons' house went dark and everyone's saying they didn't pay the bill, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it may be a power fault and it is their private business | Pass it on, since dark houses prove it | Pass it on, so people know they are poor and can't pay their bills | Pass it on, and say they were fined
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduction of electricity by wet skin? | Wet skin admits a far greater quantity of electricity into the body than dry skin | Wet skin prevents electricity from entering the body | Wet and dry skin conduct electricity equally | Wet skin conducts electricity only in winter
exam: Identify the correct method of disconnecting an appliance from a wall socket. | Holding the plug itself and drawing it out | Pulling sharply upon the cord | Twisting the cord around the hand and pulling | Kicking the plug out of the socket
exam: It being given that a power line has fallen to the ground, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To remain far away, the line possibly being live, and to inform an adult | To move the line aside with a dry stick | To approach the line in rubber shoes | To touch the line to ascertain whether it is live
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the overloading of a socket. | Too many appliances plugged into a single socket may overheat the wires and cause a fire | Several appliances in one socket save electricity | Overloading a socket merely causes the lights to dim | A socket cannot be overloaded by household appliances
